### 4. Store/DAO Layer -- Visibility Filtering in SQL

The store layer does not enforce visibility as business logic, but it supports it by translating `VisibilityList` and `Filters` (containing visibility expressions) into SQL WHERE clauses.

**SQLite**: `/bench/repo/store/db/sqlite/memo.go`, lines 96-102 -- generates `memo.visibility IN (?)` clause from `FindMemo.VisibilityList`.

**PostgreSQL**: `/bench/repo/store/db/postgres/memo.go`, lines 89-95 -- same pattern.

**MySQL**: `/bench/repo/store/db/mysql/memo.go`, lines 104-110 -- same pattern.

The `MemoFilter` string on `FindMemoRelation` (defined at `/bench/repo/store/memo_relation.go`, line 26) is compiled via the CEL filter engine and joins the `memo` table when listing relations, so that relations to invisible memos are excluded. This is used in the SQLite driver at `/bench/repo/store/db/sqlite/memo_relation.go`, line 80, PostgreSQL at `/bench/repo/store/db/postgres/memo_relation.go`, line 82, and MySQL at `/bench/repo/store/db/mysql/memo_relation.go`, line 73.

The filter schema at `/bench/repo/internal/filter/schema.go`, lines 162-172 defines `visibility` as a filterable field of type string, mapped to `memo.visibility` column, supporting `==` and `!=` operators.

---

